A Brief Evolution of Prompting & Reasoning Frameworks

Zero-Shot and Few-Shot Prompting
The earliest breakthroughs in GPT-3 era (2020–2021) were surprisingly simple: you could type in a direct question (“What is the capital of France?”) and the model would answer. That was zero-shot prompting. Soon, researchers found that providing few-shot examples — demonstrating the desired pattern with a few sample Q&As in the prompt — improved results dramatically. This was the birth of “prompt engineering.”

Chain-of-Thought (CoT)
In 2022, a new technique took hold: Chain-of-Thought prompting. Adding a short instruction like “Let’s think step by step” encouraged models to expose intermediate reasoning instead of leaping straight to an answer. Performance on reasoning-heavy tasks like grade-school math and commonsense logic improved significantly. CoT was a turning point — it showed that LLMs could be nudged toward process transparency.

Tree-of-Thoughts, ReAct, and Beyond
Building on CoT, researchers proposed Tree-of-Thoughts (ToT), where multiple reasoning paths are explored in parallel before selecting the best one, and ReAct, which interleaved reasoning with external actions like tool use or API calls. These innovations pushed models beyond static prompting into interactive cognition.

RAG — Retrieval-Augmented Generation
Meanwhile, enterprises ran into a practical problem: LLMs hallucinated facts and lacked up-to-date knowledge. The solution was Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), where models pull from external databases or search indexes before generating responses. RAG made LLMs useful in enterprise settings — answering questions with grounding in real documents.

Deep Agents — The Hierarchy of Synthetic Cognition
A Deep Agent, by contrast, is not just a loop but a hierarchical system of loops. It embodies three key traits:

Recursion — Deep Agents can break down a task into sub-tasks, generate strategies for each, and recursively spawn sub-agents to handle them. For example, “research and draft a market analysis report” might become:

Sub-agent A: gather market data.
Sub-agent B: identify competitors.
Sub-agent C: analyze trends.
Supervisor agent: integrate findings, check consistency, and polish style.

Hierarchy — Deep Agents are structured like an organization chart. Supervisory agents oversee worker agents. Some specialize in planning, others in execution, others in auditing. This mirrors human organizations, where managers delegate, auditors review, and teams specialize.

Reflection — Deep Agents don’t just act; they evaluate their own performance. They may pause mid-task, critique their own output, and revise strategies. Reflection prevents them from blindly running down a wrong path.

Together, recursion, hierarchy, and reflection transform an agent from a reactive tool into something closer to a synthetic organization — a cognitive system with structure, feedback loops, and internal governance.

The prompt engineering era taught us to talk to AI. The context engineering era is teaching us to think with AI.

